    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d412">

                <pb facs="0596" n="310" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg310"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857/190</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Germcmy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-11-18T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 18, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3589. Department advised that P. &amp; O. liner <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Arabia</hi> carrying some 480 passengers, including about 170 women and
                    children and also some Americans, was torpedoed without warning November 6 in
                    Mediterranean on her homeward trip by German or Austrian submarine. It appears
                    that the weather was fine and that the submarine, before dispatching the
                    torpedo, could have seen women and children on the deck. This Government is
                    unable to square this disaster with the German assurance of May 4, 1916, which,
                    it is understood, binds both Central powers. Please bring this case to attention
                    of Government to which you are accredited and request an immediate investigation
                    and prompt report in this case.</p>
                <p>Repeat Vienna, No. 1424.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
